      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Let $a=67$ and $n=6.$ Find positive integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n,$ and determine $q\\cdot r.$",
      "worked_solution": "Dividing $67$ by $6$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n11<67÷6<12.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=11.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by computing $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=67-(11)(6)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $67=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}67={{11}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 6+{{1}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $q\\cdot r=11\\cdot 1=11.$",
